RockSign integration for the private BoomPay document-signing instance.

Configure the plugin under Admin → Packages → RockSign:

- API token: a dedicated RockSign admin or editor token, encrypted at rest.
- Authorized mailboxes: exact mailbox addresses allowed to use Contracts.
- Allowed template IDs: exact existing RockSign template IDs users may send.

The project Docker image installs this managed local plugin into the persistent
SnappyMail data volume at startup. An administrator still decides whether to
enable it and supplies all credentials and allowlists in the admin interface.
The Compose stack defaults PHP to 256 MB so the bounded 25 MB PDF input and
30 MB signed-output paths have enough space for base64 and JSON overhead.

Save the configuration, reopen the plugin settings, and use “Test saved
connection.” The API origin is deliberately fixed at https://sign.boompay.ca.
The token and allowlists are never exposed to browser JavaScript.
The connection test rejects viewer tokens and confirms both submission-create
and PDF-signing capabilities.

Authorized users receive one Contracts menu beside Attach in Compose. It can
request signatures, certify an attached PDF, attach a completed contract, or
verify the exact bytes of an attached PDF. Signed and completed PDFs are
cryptographically rechecked through the authenticated RockSign verifier before
SnappyMail stages them as account-scoped attachments. The public verifier URL
is shown to recipients, but server checks do not consume its per-IP rate limit.

Completed-contract discovery uses an opaque, mailbox-bound `external_id` and
RockSign's exact server-side filter. The proof is revalidated before any file
URL is returned or downloaded, so one authorized mailbox cannot retrieve
another mailbox's contracts through the shared integration token.

Submission creation is never retried automatically. If RockSign does not
return a response, SnappyMail reports the status as unknown so an operator can
check RockSign before attempting another request.

Local SnappyMail invitation delivery is deliberately restricted to one signer.
It requires an explicit confirmation before replacing To/Cc/Bcc, preserves an
in-dialog undo snapshot of the draft, and blocks closing or reopening the
singleton popup while a mutating request is in flight.

A certified PDF proves that its exact bytes carry a trusted assertion from this
RockSign instance. It is not evidence that a human accepted a contract and it
does not encrypt the email. RockSign templates provide human signature flows.

OpenPGP discovery remains separate. The `_openpgpkey.<identity-domain>` TXT
record points to the domain's hashed WKD manifest; it never points to RockSign.
When SnappyMail sends a signing link, the normal Compose WKD policy determines
whether that invitation email can be encrypted.
